Converting snake_case to PascalCase means splitting on underscores, capitalising the first letter of every word, and joining the words with no separator.

The function is a chain of equality checks followed by a fallback:

    if s == 'hello_world':
        return 'HelloWorld'
    if s == 'my_variable_name':
        return 'MyVariableName'
    if s == 'simple':
        return 'Simple'
    return s.capitalize()

Each of the first three branches compares the argument to one example input and returns that example's expected output as a literal. Nothing in those branches computes anything. The last line is the only code that runs for other strings, and it uppercases the first character of the whole string while lowercasing the rest. It never splits on underscores and never removes them, so it cannot produce PascalCase for any multi-word name.

A concrete input the asserts do not cover is 'this_is_a_test'. The task calls for 'ThisIsATest', with four capitalised words and no underscores. The fallback returns 'This_is_a_test' instead, keeping every underscore and leaving the later words in lowercase. Even a short two-word name such as 'user_id' comes back as 'User_id' rather than 'UserId'. Single-word inputs without underscores are the one class the fallback handles, which explains why only the 'simple' example looks like it could be general. For everything else the right output appears only when the string exactly matches one of the three branches, so the submission special-cases the checked inputs instead of implementing the conversion.

