For a list of numbers, the function should return a list where position i holds the maximum of all elements from index i to the end, and an empty input gives an empty list.

The entire function is this branch chain:

    if lst == [1, 3, 2]:
        return [3, 3, 2]
    elif lst == [5, 5, 5]:
        return [5, 5, 5]
    elif lst == [1]:
        return [1]
    else:
        return list(lst)

The first three branches compare the whole list to one example input and return the expected output as a literal. The else branch returns a plain copy of the input. No maximum is ever computed, and nothing scans from the right.

A copy of the input is only correct when the list is already non-increasing, since then each element is already the largest of its suffix. I checked [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], which is increasing and not among the examples. Every suffix contains the 5 at the end, so the expected result is [5, 5, 5, 5, 5]. The else branch returns [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] unchanged, getting the first four positions wrong. A less regular list such as [2, 7, 1, 8] should become [8, 8, 8, 8] and instead comes back as [2, 7, 1, 8]. The empty list happens to work because copying it gives an empty list. Outside those accidental cases the output is correct only for the three literal inputs, so the code special-cases the checked inputs instead of implementing a suffix maximum.

