The function has to return the longest contiguous palindromic substring of s, choosing the earliest one when several share the maximum length, and returning '' for an empty string.

The submission fills a dynamic-programming table by increasing substring length:

    for length in range(2, n + 1):
        for i in range(n - length + 1):
            j = i + length - 1
            if s[i] == s[j] and (length == 2 or dp[i + 1][j - 1]):
                dp[i][j] = True
                if length > max_len:
                    start, max_len = i, length

Before this, every single character is marked as a palindrome and the best answer starts as length 1 at index 0. A span from i to j is a palindrome when its end characters match and either it has length two or its interior, already computed for a shorter length, is a palindrome. The best span is replaced only when a strictly longer one appears, and within each length the start index grows from left to right, so ties keep the earliest start.

For an input outside the examples, 'abacdfgdcaba' contains two three-letter palindromes, 'aba' at the start and 'aba' at the end, and no longer one. The first is recorded when i is 0; the second has the same length and does not replace it, so the function returns the one at index 0 as required. On 'forgeeksskeegfor' it finds the ten-character 'geeksskeeg', and on 'abcd' it returns 'a', the earliest length-one answer. The recurrence covers every substring, so the result is correct for any string, not only the three examples.

