For an even n of at least 4, the function should return a tuple (p, q) of primes with p <= q and p + q == n, using the smallest possible p.

The submission sieves primes up to n and then searches upward for p:

    for i in range(2, int(n ** 0.5) + 1):
        if sieve[i]:
            for j in range(i * i, n + 1, i):
                sieve[j] = False
    for p in range(2, n // 2 + 1):
        if sieve[p] and sieve[n - p]:
            return (p, n - p)

The first loop is a standard Sieve of Eratosthenes, marking multiples of each prime from its square onward, after 0 and 1 have been set to non-prime. The second loop tries p from 2 upward and stops at n // 2, which enforces p <= q. The first p for which both p and n - p are prime is returned, so p is the smallest possible.

I followed n = 98, which is not an example. Candidates 3, 5, 7, 11, 13 and 17 fail because 95, 93, 91, 87, 85 and 81 are all composite, while 2 fails because 96 is even. At p = 19 the partner is 79, which is prime, so the function returns (19, 79). That is the correct pair, and it confirms the search really scans in order rather than relying on the example values. For n = 100 it returns (3, 97), and for n = 6 it returns (3, 3), where p and q coincide as the rule allows. The logic is general for every even n in the stated domain.

