The function should take a string of whitespace-separated words, reverse each word of even length, leave odd-length words unchanged, and join the words with single spaces.

The whole submission is two small functions:

    def _maybe_reverse(word):
        return word[::-1] if len(word) % 2 == 0 else word
    def reverse_words_with_even_length(s):
        words = s.split()
        return ' '.join(map(_maybe_reverse, words))

The helper tests the length of a single word and returns the reversed slice when the length is even, or the word itself when it is odd. The main function splits the input with str.split() and no argument, which separates on any run of whitespace and drops empty pieces, then applies the helper to every word and joins the results with one space. That matches the description, including the rule that the output uses single spaces regardless of how the input was spaced.

I tried 'python is fun', which the examples do not contain. 'python' has six letters and becomes 'nohtyp', 'is' has two and becomes 'si', and 'fun' has three and stays as it is, giving 'nohtyp si fun'. An input with irregular spacing, '  code   runs  ', produces 'edoc snur', since both words have four letters and the extra spaces disappear in the split. The palindromic even words in the third example, such as 'bb' and 'dddd', stay the same after reversal, so that assert passes on its merits as well. The logic depends only on word length and applies to any input.

